TI  - RECOVERY BY THE CORAL ACROPORA-CERVICORNIS AFTER DRILLING MUD EXPOSURE - THE FREE AMINO-ACID POOL
T2  - ARCHIVES OF ENVIRONMENTAL CONTAMINATION AND TOXICOLOGY
LA  - English
AB  - Corals were exposed to drilling mud for 24 h and then allowed to recover for 48 h in clean seawater. Depending on the concentration and the mud used, exposure produced an increase or decrease in free amino acid (FAA) pool size. Aspartate was affected to a greater degree than other amino acids. No clear instance of recovery could be ascertained after 48 h in clean seawater. In several cases, corals, apparently unaffected by a 24 h exposure, suffered significant changes in the FAA pool during the  48 h recovery period. The degree of toxicity of the drilling mud could not be accurately predicted from the 24 h exposure data. In many cases, the choice of a normalizing parameter determined whether 2 sets of data were significantly different or not. Accurate effects assessment depended on a comparison of several methods of normalization to confirm statistical results.
